ENTRY            C2888   20260717   20261014   20261014       C259
SUBENT        C2888001   20260717   20261014   20261014       C259
BIB                  9         21
TITLE      Excitation function for the 6Li+alpha reaction
           between 0.5 and 1.4 MeV
AUTHOR     (A.Gula,R.J.deBoer,R.Kelmar,J.Gorres,K.V.Manukyan,
           E.Stech,W.Tan,M.Wiescher)
REFERENCE  (J,PR/C,106,065801,2022)
           #doi:10.1103/PhysRevC.106.065801
INSTITUTE  (1USANOT)
FACILITY   (VDG,1USANOT) The 5U Pelletron accelerator at the
           University of Notre Dame.
INC-SPECT  Beam intensities ranged between 1 and 15 microA
           because higher intensities resulted in rapid
           deterioration of the lithium targets.
CORRECTION Due to the very close geometry of the detector to the
           target, coincidence summing corrections were
           performed. The corrections to the Egamma = 718 keV
           yield were performed. The corrections resulted in an
           increased yield. For the Ealpha = 1168 keV resonance
           the correction was ~15%, whereas for the Ealpha = 520
           keV resonance it was only ~4%.
STATUS     (APRVD) Approved by R.J.deBoer on July 31, 2026.
HISTORY    (20260717C) BP
ENDBIB              21
NOCOMMON             0          0
ENDSUBENT           24
SUBENT        C2888002   20260717   20261014   20261014       C259
BIB                  6         21
REACTION   (3-LI-6(A,G)5-B-10,PAR,DA)
DETECTOR   (SCIN,HPGE) CeBr3 detector. Two 100% HPGe detectors,
           both n-type, coaxial, EGC 100-260-R models from
           Canberra.
SAMPLE     (3-LI-6,ENR=0.95) Target 2. 6LiF enriched to >95%,
           targets of thicknesses between ~10 and ~50microg/cm2
           were evaporated onto 0.5 mm tantalum backings.
           Targets were mounted 45 degree relative to the beam,
           making their effective thicknesses ~14 and
           71 microg/cm2, respectively. This tantalum target
           backing served as the beam stop.
METHOD     (GSPEC)
ERR-ANALYS (DATA-ERR) No complete details on uncertainties.
           (ERR-SYS) Systematic uncertainty.
           (ERR-1) Charge collection.
           (ERR-2) Stopping power.
           (ERR-3) Efficiency.
           (ERR-4) Stoichiometry.
           (ERR-5) Thick target analysis techniques.
